    "result_text": "There is only one test class in the suite, and it passes. **BUILD SUCCESS.**\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the level rules with a points system while keeping structural impact low.\n\n**What changed (3 files):**\n\n1. **`OwnerMembershipLevel.java`** \u2014 Added a small `points(owner)` method (0 base; +2 email, +1 zero `namesakeCount`, +2 household of 3+, +3 tenure over 365 days) plus a one-line private `largeHousehold` helper. Rewrote `of(owner)` to a single ternary mapping points \u2192 level (1/2/3/4). The existing `hasEmail`/`tenuredOver365` helpers were reused unchanged.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a single `@Mapping` line exposing `membershipPoints` via `OwnerMembershipLevel.points(owner)`. Crucially, I kept `of(owner)` returning the *level*, so the existing `membershipLevel` mapping line stayed untouched.\n\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the `membershipPoints` schema property and updated `membershipLevel` (max now 4, new description).\n\n**Structural-impact choices:** I kept the logic in the existing dedicated helper class rather than growing the mapper or the `Owner`/DTO classes (which carry large `WMC_other`). Every method I touched is a tiny static function with few sibling methods, and I preserved the `of(owner)` signature so only one mapper line needed adding \u2014 no changes to the high-complexity surrounding code.\n\nThe single test asserts a fresh owner with an email and no namesake scores `membershipPoints=3` \u2192 `membershipLevel=2`, which now passes.",
